# Veridian runtime state — filesystem-first, portable, never committed.
.veridian/

# Node
node_modules/
npm-debug.log*
*.tgz

# The build output. Generated by `npm run build`, never edited, and reproduced from the source tree
# on any machine - so committing it would be committing a copy that can silently disagree with the
# source it was compiled from. `npm pack` and `npm publish` run `prepare` and rebuild it.
dist/

# Playwright artifacts that escape the run bundle
test-results/
playwright-report/

# The VS Code Cockpit's compiled output. Generated by `npm run build` inside extension/vscode, never
# edited, and reproduced from the source tree - the same reasoning as dist/ above. This is the one
# directory in the repository that has a build step at all, and the reason is not a preference: the
# extension host is not Node's loader, so it cannot strip types and cannot run the `.ts` the rest of
# Veridian runs directly.
extension/vscode/out/

# The packaged Cockpit. Generated by `npm run package` inside extension/vscode, never edited, and
# reproducible from the source tree `gate` already verified - the same reasoning as `dist/` and `out/`
# above, one runtime further out. It is a zip, so a committed copy is a binary nobody can diff against
# the extension it claims to be, and the archive is the one artifact no test in this tree can load
# (there is no VS Code test host here) - which is exactly why `npm run smoke:vsix` reads it and why it
# must not be possible to commit it. The rule is the archive *type* rather than one filename, so a
# second target added to the `package` script cannot arrive unignored.
*.vsix

# The database the `inventory-db` example builds. Like `.veridian/`, it is a world this machine
# produced, not source: it is deleted and rebuilt from `app/build.mjs` on every start and every reset,
# so committing it would commit a copy that can disagree with the script that makes it. The demo's
# restore does not need to remove it - the build removes it, which is what makes a reset a reset.
examples/inventory-db/app/**.db

# The substitute image registry the `sim-k8s` example's build produces. Same reasoning as the database
# above, and one consequence on top of it: this directory is what the world's control plane reads to
# decide whether an image can be pulled, so committing it would let a manifest naming a tag the build
# never produced pull successfully - a false PASS in the world's own evidence, kept in the repository.
examples/sim-k8s/app/build/

# The sandbox tree the `sim-posix` example's world builds. Same reasoning again, with the sharper
# version of the k8s case: this directory *is* the system the run is judged against, so a committed
# copy would let a criterion read a file the run did not install, a mode the run did not record, or a
# socket nobody bound - and the bundle would then hold a PASS for a world this machine did not produce.
# It sits under `app/` because `environment.yaml` resolves `posix.root` against its own `app:` value,
# so the tree the world creates and the tree this rule excludes are the same one by construction.
examples/sim-posix/app/sandbox/

# The sandbox tree the `sim-os` example's world builds. The reasoning is the `sim-posix` one exactly,
# and it is worth stating separately rather than as a glob over both, because the *thing* is different:
# this tree is not a POSIX filesystem with modes, it is a tree plus the security record, the account
# list, the configuration store and the service definitions the world holds about it. A committed copy
# would let a criterion read a registry value the run never wrote, an access decision the run never
# reached, or a service the world never registered - and the bundle would hold a PASS for a system this
# machine did not build. It sits under `app/` because `environment.yaml` resolves `os.root` against its
# own `app:` value, so the tree the world creates and the tree this rule excludes are the same one by
# construction.
examples/sim-os/app/sandbox/

# The sandbox tree the `sim-container` example's world builds. The reasoning is the `sim-posix` and
# `sim-os` one, with a third thing in it: this tree is not only a filesystem, it is the image store
# (each image's record plus a copy of the build context it was built from), the container records, the
# volume roots and the world's own call log. A committed copy would let a criterion read an image the
# run never built, a container record the run never created, or a call the run never made - and the
# bundle would hold a PASS for a runtime this machine did not stand up. It sits under `app/` because
# `core/environment/load.ts` resolves `container.root` against this document's own `app:` value, so the
# tree the world creates and the tree this rule excludes are the same one by construction.
examples/sim-container/app/sandbox/

# The build context the `sim-container` application generates. This one is not the world's state, it is
# the application's *output*: `provision.mjs` derives the Dockerfile and the four programs from its own
# arrays on every run, and the world copies this directory into each image it builds. It is excluded
# for the reason every generated tree in this repository is - `dist/`, `extension/vscode/out/` and
# `sim-k8s/app/build/` all carry it - and for one more that is particular to this world: a committed
# context would be a *second* copy of the four programs, and a defect injected into the derived copy
# instead of the file it is derived from would be erased by the next iteration's own provisioning, so
# the demo would report a repair the world never observed.
examples/sim-container/app/context/

# The sandbox tree the `sim-vscode` example's world builds. The reasoning is the `sim-posix`,
# `sim-os` and `sim-container` one, and the thing stored in it is different again: this tree is the
# installed extension directory, the workspace folder, and - because extensions are stateful across the
# host processes that load them - the durable store an extension writes through `globalState` and
# `workspaceState`. A committed copy would let a criterion read a command registration the run never
# made, a workspace file the run never wrote, or a stored value the run never saved, and the bundle
# would then hold a PASS for a host this machine did not stand up. It sits under `app/` because
# `core/environment/load.ts` resolves `vscode.root` against this document's own `app:` value, so the
# tree the world creates and the tree this rule excludes are the same one by construction.
examples/sim-vscode/app/sandbox/

# The sandbox tree the `sim-mobile` example's world builds. The reasoning is the `sim-posix`,
# `sim-os`, `sim-container` and `sim-vscode` one, and what is stored in it is the handset: the device
# record with its boot state and geometry, the bundle store holding a real copy of every installed
# tree, the permission decisions, the deep-link registrations, the notification queue, the keychain
# entries and the captured log streams. A committed copy would let a criterion read a bundle the run
# never installed, a permission the run never granted, a notification the run never posted, or a
# keychain entry the run never set - and the bundle would then hold a PASS for a device this machine
# did not stand up. It sits under `app/` because `core/environment/load.ts` resolves `mobile.root`
# against this document's own `app:` value, so the tree the world creates and the tree this rule
# excludes are the same one by construction.
examples/sim-mobile/app/sandbox/

# The bundle sources the `sim-mobile` application generates. This one is not the world's state, it is
# the application's *output*: `provision.mjs` derives the manifest and the four bundle files from its
# own arrays on every run, and `bundle install` copies this directory into the store. It is excluded
# for the reason every generated tree in this repository is - `dist/`,
# `extension/vscode/out/`, `sim-k8s/app/build/`, `sim-container/app/context/` all carry it - and for
# one more this world shares with `sim-container`: a committed copy would be a *second* copy of the
# files, and a defect injected into the derived copy instead of the array it is derived from would be
# erased by the next iteration's own provisioning, so the demo would report a repair the world never
# observed.
examples/sim-mobile/app/context/

# The sandbox tree the `local-process` example's world builds. The reasoning is the `sim-posix`,
# `sim-os`, `sim-container` and `sim-vscode` one, and what is stored in it is the plainest thing any of
# them stores: a release the application actually wrote - `dist/`, a manifest and a few files - plus the
# files an `add` command really created. The difference this world adds is that the program and the tree
# are on the *same* real filesystem, so a committed copy would let a criterion read the files of a run
# that is over before this one started. That is exactly the defect the tenth world's own port was fixed
# for (a run inheriting a tree an earlier run built), and an ignore rule is the cheapest half of holding
# it. It sits under `app/` because `core/environment/load.ts` resolves `process.root` against this
# document's own `app:` value, so the tree the world creates and the tree this rule excludes are the
# same one by construction. The `**` is not decoration: before the adapter resolved its root once, the
# program was handed a root-relative path while its working directory was the application's own, and a
# run wrote a whole sandbox at `app/examples/local-process/app/sandbox/` - which `git add -A` staged,
# because the rule named one spelling of the tree and the defect produced another. The glob covers the
# nesting, so a regression cannot commit a second sandbox while its cause is being found.
examples/local-process/app/**/sandbox/

# The compiled Cockpit the `vscode-cockpit` example stages, and this is the one generated tree in the
# repository that is a *copy of another generated tree*. `demo.ts` copies `extension/vscode/out/` (plus
# the manifest, the icon and the licence, which is the manifest's own `files` allowlist) into
# `app/extension/` before the run and removes it afterwards, because `resolveHostPath` refuses a path
# outside the application directory - so the artifact has to sit inside the world's own app tree to be
# installed at all. It is not committed for the reason `extension/vscode/out/` is not: it is a build
# product, and a committed copy could disagree with the source it was compiled from. `--keep-staged`
# deliberately leaves it behind for inspection, which is precisely when this rule matters.
examples/vscode-cockpit/app/extension/

# The sandbox tree the `vscode-cockpit` example's world builds. The reasoning is the `sim-vscode` one
# exactly - the installed extension directory, the workspace folder and the durable store an extension
# writes through `globalState` and `workspaceState` - and it is stated separately rather than shared
# because the two worlds keep separate trees. It sits under `app/` because `core/environment/load.ts`
# resolves `vscode.root` against this document's own `app:` value, so the tree the world creates and the
# tree this rule excludes are the same one by construction.
examples/vscode-cockpit/app/sandbox/

# The sandbox tree the `environment-twin` example's world builds, and this rule was added *after* the
# tree was already committed - which is the whole reason it is worth stating. The reasoning is the
# `local-process` one: the probe and the tree are on the same real filesystem, so a committed copy
# would let a criterion read the artifacts of a run that is over before this one started - and the
# artifacts here are worse than a stale release, because one of them is a *capability reading*. A
# committed `twin/capabilities.json` would be a document describing whatever machine first ran the
# example, sitting inside the sandbox where the next reader would find it looking like current
# evidence. That is the same shape as the `sim-mobile` context rule above, one layer further out.
#
# The two files it cost are `app/sandbox/capability-probe-proof.txt` and
# `app/sandbox/twin/capabilities.json`, staged by `git add examples/environment-twin` on the commit
# that landed the example, because a standalone probe run had written them into the tree before the
# world ever created its own. The `**` is copied from the `local-process` rule deliberately: that
# example's rule needed it after a root-resolution defect wrote a *nested* sandbox, and the same
# defect was found here in the same shape, so this rule covers the nesting from the start rather than
# waiting to pay for it again.
examples/environment-twin/app/**/sandbox/

# The sandbox tree the `workspace-audit` example's world builds, added *with* the example rather than
# after it - which is the one way this rule differs from its neighbour's two lines up, and the reason
# it is worth saying so. `environment-twin` paid for a late rule; this one is the same rule written
# before the same mistake could be made, and the `**` is carried across for the same reason it was
# carried there.
#
# The artifacts here are a specific hazard rather than a stale release, and it is a new kind. This
# example's subject is the **checkout it is running in**, so a committed `audit/workspace.json` would be
# a document describing this repository - branch, manifest, top-level census - sitting inside the
# sandbox where the next reader would find it looking like a current reading of *their* checkout. That
# is the `sim-mobile` context rule and the `environment-twin` capability rule arriving at a third
# subject: a committed reading of a live thing stops being a reading and becomes a claim.
#
# The sandbox is also the only path this world may write at all. Every write outside it is refused by
# the runtime, so an unignored sandbox is the one artefact a run *can* leave in the tree - which makes
# this rule the whole of the containment rather than a hygiene measure beside it.
examples/workspace-audit/app/**/sandbox/

# The sandbox tree the `env-reality` example's world builds, added with the example - which is now the
# third rule written *before* the mistake rather than after it, and the pattern `**`/`sandbox/` is
# carried across for the reasons its two neighbours state.
#
# The artifact this one excludes is a **reading of the operator's environment**, and that makes it the
# sharpest of the three rather than one more of them. The probe writes its census to
# `app/sandbox/env/reading.json`, containing the number of variables it could see, how many were
# credential-shaped, how many held anything, and whether one named variable was present. Committed, that
# is a document about the machine of whoever last ran the demo, sitting in the tree where the next reader
# would find it looking like a current reading of their own - the `sim-mobile` context rule and the
# `environment-twin` capability rule at a fourth subject.
#
# It is deliberately a **count** and never a value, which is why this is a hygiene rule and not the whole
# of the containment: `core/environment/env-crawl.ts` makes a leak unrepresentable in the type, so what a
# committed sandbox would expose is a figure about a machine rather than a secret from it. The containment
# is held by the crawler; this rule stops a stale figure from being read as a fresh one.
examples/env-reality/app/**/sandbox/

# The state directory the self-acceptance contract's world builds - the one generated tree in this
# repository that is not under an example's `app/`. The reasoning is the `sim-posix` one, and it is
# sharper here because the world's root and the CLI's `--state-dir` are the *same* directory:
# `acceptance/environment.yaml` declares `app: ..` and `process.root: sandbox`, so the tree the world
# creates, the tree this rule excludes and the directory `init` writes into are one path by
# construction. That is what `#rebuild` removes between iterations, and it is the only reason a second
# invocation of the contract starts from an empty tree instead of finding the first invocation's
# `config.yaml` in place - where AC-002's own `init` would refuse and a criterion would report a
# defect against a program that was correct.
#
# The rule is anchored to the repository root rather than spelled `sandbox/`, and the anchor is not
# tidiness: an unanchored pattern matches a directory of that name at any depth, which would silence a
# future world that tracks one on purpose. This contract is the only thing that writes here.
/sandbox/

# Editors / OS
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db
*.swp
